<section class="projectsection dark">
    <div class="container">
        <div class="row">
  
            <div class="images col-md-4">
                <img class="image" src="/images/projects/sbux/p2t_lockup_min@2x.png" />
            </div>

            <div class="imagemeta col-md-7">
                <h3>Summary</h3>
               <p>At Starbucks I was asked to help <strong>retire a piece of beloved but expensive and frequently broken hardware</strong> - barcode scanners - used by employees to take inventory and thaw food items for the next day.</p>
               <p>As a former restaurant worker still obsessed with cooking, the task was near and dear to my heart, and also gave me a chance to work in a medium I enjoy - apps people use to do their jobs. The opposite of another Candy Crush clone hoping to keep us obsessed with our screens, I love digital products that help people do things in the real world, and are built with an awareness that they're <strong>a tool, not a goal unto themselves.</strong></p> 
               
               <p>Back of house software can also be a company's <strong>most frequent employee touchpoint</strong> and so I value it deeply as an opportunity to show someone whether you understand their day to day and are prioritizing giving them the best tools possible.</p>
            </div>


        </div>   
    </div>
</section>

<section class="projectsection">
    <div class="container">
        <div class="row">


            <div class="images col-md-3">
                <img class="image" src="/images/projects/sbux/sbux_rack.png" />
            </div>


            <div class="imagemeta col-md-6">
                <h3>Design challenge</h3>
                <p>The sentiment when I came on board and saw the hastily-ported over version of the app running on the iPad amounted to:</p> 
                <p class="pquote">"They're going to hate it, and we're sure it's going to be slower. Just do what you can."</p>
                <p>The initial iPad app was not only cumbersome to use, its not-optimized interface required users to stretch their fingers to difficult touch areas, read tiny text at a further viewing distance than the UI was designed for, and manually search through lists of text to find and count products, all in the middle of a cramped area full of hot, sharp and heavy things. In short, it was an accessibility and usability nightmare.</p>
            </div>
            
            <div class="images col-md-3">
                <img class="image" src="/images/projects/sbux/p2t_ipad.png" />
            </div>

        </div>   
    </div>
</section>

<section class="projectsection dark">
    <div class="container">
        <div class="row">
  
           <div class="images col-md-2 col-md-offset-1">
                <img class="image" src="/images/projects/sbux/docs.png" />
            </div>

           <div class="imagemeta wide col-md-5">
                <h3>Research &amp; Ideation</h3>
                <p>In my time with the team, I:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li>Ran studies <strong>quantifying exactly how much speed loss</strong> was occurring without scanner hardware.</li>
                    <li>Interviewed employees and conducted site visits to uncover <strong>found-in-the wild solutions</strong> that were already in use (under the radar) when scanners broke.</li>
                    <li>Annoyed everyone by obessively <strong>publishing and evangelizing</strong> findings and showing not just incremental changes but ideas for making the new app actively better than its predecessor.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <div class="images col-md-4">
                <img class="image" src="/images/projects/sbux/p2t_ipad_versions.png" />
            </div>


        </div>   
    </div>
</section>

<section class="projectsection">
    <div class="container">
        <div class="row">

            <div class="images col-md-3">
                <img class="image" src="/images/projects/sbux/goggles.png" />
            </div>

            <div class="imagemeta wide col-md-5">
                <h3>Prototyping &amp; testing</h3>
                <p>The team had access to an amazing test facility - an actual mock kitchen where we could run tests with employees <strong>counting real products in spaces similar to where they'd be used,</strong> as well as eye tracking goggles that gave additional insights into the <strong>cognitive and physical details of the processes.</strong></p>  
                <p>Having outgrown the capabilities of Figma prototypes, and needing to get an accurate apples-to-apples speed comparison, we used <strong>code prototyping</strong> to test out different parts of the process and prove which elements of designs improved on key metrics.</p>
                <p>As testing continued, we were able to <strong>quantify the cost of each change</strong> and (since we were talking about 10k stores worth of employees) the overall <strong>potential impact.</strong></p>
            </div>

            <div class="images col-md-4">
                <img class="image" src="/images/projects/sbux/testing_lockup.png" />
            </div>

        </div>   
    </div>
</section>

<section class="projectsection dark">
    <div class="container">
        <div class="row">

            <div class="imagemeta wide col-md-6 col-md-offset-1">
                <h3>Outcomes</h3>
               <ul>
                    <li>The redesigned app and process demonstrated a <strong>60% speed increase</strong> over scanner-based counting.</li>
                    <li>Users expressed positive sentiments towards the iPad versions and the new workflow, and the <strong>gamification elements</strong> introduced to help make a laborious process feel satisfying.</li>
                    <li>The company <strong>chose to devote additional resources</strong> to developing the app beyond its original scope, while also choosing to <strong>let employees have greater choice</strong> in how they conducted the count process.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <div class="images col-md-4">
                <img class="image" src="/images/projects/sbux/p2t_interviews@2x.png" />
            </div>

        </div>   
    </div>
</section>

